	-> ip : used with different arguments...
		- Displays and manages IP addresses and network interfaces
		- Used to configure routing tables and network rules
		- Supports both IPv4 and IPv6 networking
		- Provides detailed and real-time network information
		- commonly used 'ip addr show', it replaces older networking tools like ifconfig and route
		
	-> iwconfig : just like ifconfig, it shows the wireless connections of yr system
	
	-> ss(socket statistic) : advanced version of netstat
	
	-> dig : detailed info about specific domain
	
	-> whois : more detailed then dig
	
	-> arp : shwo the mac address of any system
	
	-> ifplugstatus : show plugged or unplugged status of interface
	
	-> curl : get data through the API
		- ex : curl -X get [link of what u want to download] | jq (get - get that inserted link, jq - use to display structured output of curl)
	
	-> wget : it used to download anything from the internet
	
	-> iptables : iptables is a Linux command-line firewall tool used to control and secure network traffic by defining packet-filtering rules
		- ex : Imagine you want to block SSH from a specific IP but allow all other traffic: iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 22 -s 192.168.1.100 -j DROP

	-> watch : it is used to repeatedly executes a specified command at regular intervals and displays the output in the terminal (watch top)
	
	-> nmap : display open ports(with ip) of any web (nmap -v google.com) 
	
	-> route : show route table, This command helps you to view, add, or delete routes for specific destinations
	
	-> find : find directory or ... - find -type d -name leo 
	
	
	-> AWK : AWK is a powerful, data-driven text-processing language and command-line tool built into Linux and Unix systems, used for pattern scanning, data extraction, and report generation
		- in simple words awk give the permission of programming language
		- awk '{print $2,$3}' logs.log (will display column number 2 and 3)
		- awk '/DEBUG/ {print $3}' logs.log (will display 3rd column which have DEBUG in it)
		- awk 'NR>=2 && NR>=10 {print $3}' logs.log (will display the column3 with the row that is given.. NR )
		- it only supports formatted data (otherwise use sed) 
		- awk works on structure (sed works line by line)
	 
	 
	-> SED : same as awk but works on structured and semi structured and all
		- sed -n '/INFO/p' logs.log (prints all line which have 'INFO')
		- sed  's/INFO/11111/g' logs.log (replace INFO with 11111, s means string, g means globaly)
		- sed -n -e '/INFO/=' -e '/INFO/p' logs.log (= is used to print the number of line which have INFO and another expression is to print line which have INFO)
		- sed '1,20 s/INFO/11111/g' logs.log (only in 1 to 20 line the change from INFO to 11111 will be made)
		- sed '1,3p; 4q' logs.log (prints that number of lines only)
		
		
	-> GREP : grep -i info logs.log
		- -w word count
		- -n display output with number of line
		- -v used for reverse search of any word
		
		
	-> LVM : pv, vg, lv
		- lv,pv extend
		- resize file system
		- use -r to automatically resize file system
		
		





